</div>

<p>Into that vacuum stepped T. Rex, a onetime avant-garde folk duo reinvented as a straight-ahead rock band bent on reviving that genre as it rowdily was before the Beatles came along. When they hit it big, other acts imitated not just their bluesy sound, but the androgynous, brazenly fabrication-forward image of frontman Marc Bolan. Practically overnight, the name of the game changed from earnest self-expression to outlandish self-creation.</p>
<p>Other bands reshaped by this glam rock movement, as it was soon dubbed in the British media, included Slade, The Sweet, and Mott the Hoople. “My brother’s back at home with his Beatles and his Stones,” sang that last outfit in <a href="https://youtu.be/L774jUH1cq4?si=B1QU_0fvGuAofMQ-">their breakout hit</a>. “We never got it off on that revolution stuff.” That song, “All the Young Dudes,” had been written by the single most defining glam-rock figure: <a href="https://youtu.be/ToXGVcrgNZA?si=CbAZA-U-v7zgw_gh">David Bowie</a>, who himself shot to prominence in the thoroughly anti-Sixties guise of <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2012/09/ithe_story_of_ziggy_stardusti_how_david_bowie_created_the_character_that_made_him_famous.html">Ziggy Stardust</a>, the bisexual, synthetics-clad rocker from outer space who swaggered as if already famous across the universe. Within a few years, Bowie’s antenna for the zeitgeist would have him <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2018/06/david-bowie-sings-i-got-you-babe-with-marianne-faithfull-in-his-very-last-performance-as-ziggy-stardust-1973.html">retiring Ziggy Stardust and ditching glam rock</a>, a genre whose flame burned as brilliantly — and indeed garishly — as it did briefly. But in pop music, its characteristic lack of sociopolitical ideals, obsession with fame, and casual relationship with reality were here to stay.</p>
